    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. one more thing—i'm finding the whole process of keeping track of all these different rules and sources kind of chaotic. it feels like there's so much tiny, messy detail to manage that it's easy to lose track of the actual content. do you have any tips for staying organized so the citations don't just become a cluttered mess?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Organizing citations can indeed be daunting, especially with all the nuances in various citation styles. Here are some tips to help keep everything orderly:\n\n1. Use a citation manager: Tools like Zotero, Mendeley, or EndNote can help you manage your references, format your citations, and automatically generate your bibliography in the correct style.\n\n2. Create a consistent citation sheet: As you find sources for your review, create a spreadsheet or document with a standardized format for each entry. List the authors, year, title, and publication details so you can easily sort and locate them later.\n\n3. Develop a consistent naming convention: Assign a short, unique name to each source that combines the author(s) and title or other key identifiers, making it simpler to remember and reference them.\n\n4. Keep your references grouped by topic: Organize your reference list or citations by themes or categories related to your paper’s content. This might require you to do a bit of retracing your steps when you segment your findings, but it simplifies the insertion of citations.\n\n5. Regularly update your reference list: Keeping your references organized as you write goes a long way toward preventing errors and reducing stress. Update your reference list as your research progresses.\n\nUtilize these",
      "source": "target",
